.TH "ITERATOR.ZIPKEYED" "3JS" "September 4, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Iterator.zipKeyed \- Iterator.zipKeyed()
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]Iterator.zipKeyed()\f[B]\f[R] static method creates a new
\f[CR]Iterator\f[R] object that aggregates elements from multiple
iterable objects by yielding objects containing elements at the same
position, with keys specified by the input.
It essentially \(lqzips\(rq the input iterables together, allowing
simultaneous iteration over them.
.PP
The \f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R] method is similar, but yields arrays
instead of objects.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
Iterator.zipKeyed(iterables)
Iterator.zipKeyed(iterables, options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]iterables\f[R]
An object.
Each property\(cqs key is used as the key in the resulting objects.
The property\(cqs value must implement either the iterable protocol or,
failing that, the iterator protocol.
These iterables may be infinite.
Strings are rejected: to zip strings, convert them to iterators
explicitly using \f[CR]Iterator.from()\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object specifying behavior in case of inconsistent input lengths.
It may have the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]mode\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
One of the following:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(dqshortest\(dq\f[R] (default): The resulting iterator stops when
one input iterable is exhausted.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(dqlongest\(dq\f[R]: The resulting iterator stops when all input
iterables are exhausted.
Missing values from shorter iterables are filled according to the
\f[CR]padding\f[R] option.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(dqstrict\(dq\f[R]: A \f[CR]TypeError\f[R] is thrown if not all
input iterables finish at the same time.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]padding\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object.
Only retrieved and validated when \f[CR]mode\f[R] is
\f[CR]\(dqlongest\(dq\f[R].
If \f[CR]undefined\f[R] or absent, missing values from shorter iterables
are filled with \f[CR]undefined\f[R] (which is equivalent to passing an
empty object).
If an object is provided, each key of the \f[CR]iterables\f[R] argument
is retrieved \f[I]as soon as \f[CI]Iterator.zipKeyed()\f[I] is
called\f[R].
\f[CR]padding[key]\f[R] is used for missing values for
\f[CR]iterables[key]\f[R].
If the \f[CR]padding\f[R] object is missing some keys, those keys are
filled with \f[CR]undefined\f[R].
.RE
.SS Return value
A new \f[CR]Iterator\f[R] object.
Each of its elements is an object with the same keys as the
\f[CR]iterables\f[R] argument, containing the elements from each input
iterable at the corresponding position.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]Iterator.zipKeyed()\f[R] function behaves like
\f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R]; the only difference is that you can specify
the keys used in the resulting objects, while \f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R]
always uses numeric indices (by yielding arrays).
.PP
If we represent iterables as arrays, the input may looks like this:
.IP
.EX
({
  a: [a1, a2, a3, a4],
  b: [b1, b2, b3],
  c: [c1, c2, c3, c4, c5],
});
.EE
.PP
The resulting iterator, regardless of the options, will start by
yielding the following arrays:
.IP
.EX
({ a: a1, b: b1, c: c1 });
({ a: a2, b: b2, c: c2 });
({ a: a3, b: b3, c: c3 });
.EE
.PP
After the first three objects are yielded, the input iterable
\f[CR]b\f[R] is exhausted on the fourth \f[CR]next()\f[R] call\(emit
returns \f[CR]{ done: true }\f[R].
What happens next depends on the \f[CR]mode\f[R] option.
If \f[CR]mode\f[R] is \f[CR]\(dqshortest\(dq\f[R] (the default), the
resulting iterator stops here: the other two input iterators are closed.
If \f[CR]mode\f[R] is \f[CR]\(dqstrict\(dq\f[R], an error is thrown
because the other two iterables are \f[I]not\f[R] finished when the
second one yields the result \f[CR]{ done: true }\f[R].
If \f[CR]mode\f[R] is \f[CR]\(dqlongest\(dq\f[R], the resulting iterator
continues yielding objects, filling missing values.
For example, if \f[CR]padding\f[R] is not provided, it defaults to
\f[CR]undefined\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
({ a: a4, b: \f[B]undefined\f[R], c: c4 });
({ a: \f[B]undefined\f[R], b: \f[B]undefined\f[R], c: c5 });
.EE
.PP
If \f[CR]padding\f[R] is provided as an object, it may look like
\f[CR]{ a: p1, b: p2, c: p3 }\f[R].
Then \f[CR]p2\f[R] is used to fill the missing value from the input
iterable \f[CR]b\f[R], and \f[CR]p1\f[R] is used to fill the missing
value from the input iterable \f[CR]a\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
({ a: a4, b: p2, c: c4 });
({ a: p1, b: p2, c: c5 });
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Transposing tabular data
There are two common ways to represent tabular data: as an object where
each property is a column, or as an array of objects where each object
is a row.
This example shows how you can iterate the column\-based representation
by rows using \f[CR]Iterator.zipKeyed()\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] table = {
  name: [\(dqCaroline\(dq, \(dqDanielle\(dq, \(dqEvelyn\(dq],
  age: [30, 25, 35],
  city: [\(dqNew York\(dq, \(dqLondon\(dq, \(dqHong Kong\(dq],
};

\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] { name, age, city } \f[B]of\f[R] Iterator.zipKeyed(table)) {
  console.log(\(ga${name}, aged ${age}, lives in ${city}.\(ga);
}

\f[I]// Output:\f[R]
\f[I]// Caroline, aged 30, lives in New York.\f[R]
\f[I]// Danielle, aged 25, lives in London.\f[R]
\f[I]// Evelyn, aged 35, lives in Hong Kong.\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Most use cases of \f[CR]Iterator.zipKeyed()\f[R] are identical to those
of \f[CR]Iterator.zip()\f[R].
Which one to use depends on whether you already have an object of
iterables (use \f[CR]zipKeyed()\f[R]) or an array of iterables (use
\f[CR]zip()\f[R]).
We recommend using \f[CR]zipKeyed()\f[R] when possible, because
specifying explicit keys makes it harder to accidentally mix up the
order of iterables.
